=== federation versus OIDC exposure ===
$ sed -n '15,18p;52,57p' docs/setup.md
Workload Identity Federation (WIF) lets the action authenticate to the Claude API by exchanging the workflow's GitHub Actions OIDC token for a short-lived Anthropic access token — no `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret to create, store, or rotate.
...
- The workflow must grant `id-token: write` permission so the action can fetch a GitHub OIDC token.
- Do not set `anthropic_api_key` or `claude_code_oauth_token` alongside the federation inputs — a static credential takes precedence and federation will not be used.

$ curl -fsSL https://flatt.tech/research/posts/poisoning-claude-code-one-github-issue-to-break-the-supply-chain/ | rg -n -i 'credentials required to request that OIDC token|replicate this process'
The ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_TOKEN and ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL environment variables are the credentials required to request that OIDC token, so an attacker who obtains them can replicate the entire token exchange process.
With the exfiltrated ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_TOKEN and ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL, an attacker can replicate this process and obtain an installation token for the target repository.
exit: 0

Result: the decision's correction is supported. Federation removes the stored
provider credential but requires `id-token: write`; it does not remove the
OIDC request credentials described by the disclosure.

=== second security chain ===
$ curl -fsSL https://flatt.tech/research/posts/poisoning-claude-code-one-github-issue-to-break-the-supply-chain/ | rg -n -i 'fixed them as of Claude Code GitHub Actions v1.0.94|Variants of the misconfiguration issues|rated these vulnerabilities 7.8|disallowing GitHub Apps|disabled the summary section|scrub environment variables|ignoring issues and comments edited'
Variants of the misconfiguration issues described in this article were actively exploited in the wild before this article was published.
Anthropic has acknowledged these vulnerabilities and fixed them as of Claude Code GitHub Actions v1.0.94.
Anthropic was very responsive and addressed these issues by disallowing GitHub Apps from triggering workflows by default.
They also disabled the summary section, fixed other vectors for data exfiltration, and decided to scrub environment variables from the child processes spawned by Claude Code.
Additionally, they implemented a mitigation for the issue editing vector by ignoring issues and comments edited after the workflow is triggered.
They rated these vulnerabilities 7.8 under CVSS v4.0.
exit: 0

Result: S2's revision-3 history is supported: 7.8 CVSS v4.0, exploited
variants, and fixed as of v1.0.94 without a GitHub advisory. The recommended
v1.0.220 is above v1.0.94 and S1's 1.0.74. The candidate also leaves the S2
Phase-1 `allowed_non_write_users: "*"` precondition absent. The disclosure's
scrub remediation is present in action source but inactive for this candidate,
as shown above.

